('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Asthma — Diagnosis & Management','easy','mcq','A 19-year-old student with known asthma uses his salbutamol inhaler more than twice a week. He has no nocturnal symptoms and his lung function is normal. According to GINA guidelines, which step of treatment does he currently require?','["A. Step 1 — as-needed SABA only","B. Step 2 — low-dose ICS daily","C. Step 3 — low-dose ICS plus LABA","D. Step 4 — medium-dose ICS plus LABA"]','B','GINA Step 2 is indicated when symptoms occur more than twice per week but less than daily, with normal lung function. A regular low-dose inhaled corticosteroid (ICS) such as budesonide is added. Step 1 is for infrequent symptoms; Step 3 and 4 are for more severe or uncontrolled disease.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','GINA Global Strategy for Asthma Management and Prevention 2023'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Asthma — Diagnosis & Management','easy','mcq','A 32-year-old woman is diagnosed with asthma. Spirometry shows FEV1/FVC of 0.65 pre-bronchodilator, improving to 0.78 after salbutamol with a 15% rise in FEV1 and an absolute increase of 250 ml. Which spirometric criterion confirms reversible airflow obstruction?','["A. FEV1/FVC <0.7 post-bronchodilator","B. FEV1/FVC <0.7 pre-bronchodilator only","C. FEV1 improvement ≥12% AND ≥200 ml after bronchodilator","D. FVC improvement ≥15% after bronchodilator"]','C','Reversibility in asthma is defined as an improvement in FEV1 of ≥12% AND ≥200 ml after a short-acting bronchodilator. This distinguishes asthma (reversible) from COPD (largely irreversible). The FEV1/FVC <0.7 post-bronchodilator is the criterion used to confirm fixed airflow obstruction in COPD, not to demonstrate reversibility.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Asthma — Diagnosis & Management','easy','mcq','A baker presents to clinic with worsening wheeze and cough that improves on weekends and holidays. His symptoms began three years after starting his current job. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?','["A. Occupational asthma due to flour dust","B. Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis","C. Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease","D. Hypersensitivity pneumonitis"]','A','Occupational asthma due to flour dust (a high-molecular-weight sensitiser) is a classic cause of work-related asthma. The hallmark is improvement of symptoms away from work (weekends, holidays) and worsening on return. Isocyanates (paint sprayers) are the most common low-molecular-weight cause. COPD would not show this work-related pattern.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Oxford Handbook of Respiratory Medicine 3rd Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Asthma — Diagnosis & Management','medium','mcq','A 28-year-old woman with asthma develops worsening wheeze after starting naproxen for a headache. She also has chronic rhinosinusitis and nasal polyps. What is the underlying mechanism of her bronchospasm?','["A. Inhibition of COX-1 shunting arachidonic acid towards leukotriene synthesis","B. IgE-mediated type I hypersensitivity to naproxen","C. Beta-adrenergic blockade increasing bronchomotor tone","D. Direct mast cell degranulation by the NSAID molecule"]','A','Samter''s triad (aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease) consists of asthma, nasal polyps, and sensitivity to NSAIDs/aspirin. The mechanism is COX-1 inhibition, which diverts arachidonic acid away from prostaglandin synthesis towards the 5-lipoxygenase pathway, producing excess cysteinyl leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4) that cause bronchoconstriction. This is a pharmacological, not an IgE-mediated, reaction.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Asthma — Diagnosis & Management','medium','mcq','A 45-year-old man with severe persistent asthma is on high-dose ICS plus LABA but continues to have frequent exacerbations. His total serum IgE is 420 IU/mL and skin prick tests are positive to house dust mite. Which add-on biologic therapy is most appropriate?','["A. Mepolizumab","B. Benralizumab","C. Omalizumab","D. Dupilumab"]','C','Omalizumab is an anti-IgE monoclonal antibody (GINA Step 5 add-on) indicated for moderate-to-severe allergic asthma with elevated total IgE and evidence of perennial allergen sensitisation. It reduces exacerbation rates and oral steroid use. Mepolizumab and benralizumab target the IL-5 pathway and are used for severe eosinophilic asthma. Dupilumab targets IL-4Rα (IL-4/IL-13 pathway).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','BTS/SIGN British Guideline on the Management of Asthma 2019 (updated 2023)'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Asthma — Diagnosis & Management','medium','mcq','A 22-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department with severe acute asthma. Despite 10 puffs of salbutamol via spacer and 40 mg prednisolone, her PEFR remains at 35% of predicted after 30 minutes. What is the next most appropriate treatment?','["A. IV magnesium sulphate 1.2–2 g over 20 minutes","B. IV aminophylline infusion","C. Nebulised budesonide","D. Intravenous hydrocortisone 100 mg"]','A','Intravenous magnesium sulphate (1.2–2 g over 20 minutes) is recommended in acute severe asthma that has not responded adequately to initial bronchodilator therapy. It acts as a bronchodilator by inhibiting calcium-mediated smooth muscle contraction. IV aminophylline has a narrow therapeutic index and is second-line. Hydrocortisone is appropriate but would already be initiated with prednisolone; nebulised budesonide is not a first-line acute treatment.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','BTS/SIGN British Guideline on the Management of Asthma 2019 (updated 2023)'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Asthma — Diagnosis & Management','medium','mcq','A 30-year-old with asthma presents with increasing breathlessness, wheeze, and a PEFR of 42% of best. His SpO2 is 94% on air. Arterial blood gas shows pH 7.44, PaCO2 3.8 kPa, PaO2 9.1 kPa. How should this attack be classified?','["A. Mild asthma exacerbation","B. Moderate asthma exacerbation","C. Acute severe asthma","D. Life-threatening asthma"]','C','Acute severe asthma is defined by any of: PEFR 33–50% of best, respiratory rate ≥25/min, heart rate ≥110/min, or inability to complete sentences. This patient''s PEFR of 42% meets this criterion. Life-threatening features require PEFR <33%, SpO2 <92%, silent chest, cyanosis, poor respiratory effort, bradycardia, or a normal/rising PaCO2. This patient''s PaCO2 is appropriately low (hyperventilating), excluding life-threatening status.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','BTS/SIGN British Guideline on the Management of Asthma 2019 (updated 2023)'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Asthma — Diagnosis & Management','medium','mcq','A 35-year-old hypertensive asthmatic patient is started on a new medication by his cardiologist. Two weeks later he presents with worsening wheeze and reduced exercise tolerance. Which drug is most likely responsible?','["A. Amlodipine","B. Ramipril","C. Atenolol","D. Furosemide"]','C','Beta-blockers (including cardioselective agents like atenolol) can precipitate bronchoconstriction in asthma patients by blocking beta-2 receptors in bronchial smooth muscle. They are contraindicated in asthma (but used with caution in COPD). Ramipril can cause a dry cough (due to bradykinin accumulation) but does not typically cause bronchospasm. Amlodipine and furosemide are safe in asthma.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Asthma — Diagnosis & Management','hard','mcq','A 25-year-old with known asthma presents with acute wheeze. Her PEFR is 28% of best. On examination, the chest is silent with no audible wheeze. Her SpO2 is 89% and her heart rate is 58 bpm. ABG shows pH 7.31, PaCO2 6.8 kPa. Which combination of features most clearly classifies this as life-threatening asthma?','["A. Silent chest, bradycardia, and rising PaCO2","B. PEFR <33% and SpO2 <92%","C. Tachycardia and inability to complete sentences","D. PEFR <33% and respiratory alkalosis"]','A','Life-threatening features of acute asthma include: silent chest, cyanosis, poor respiratory effort, bradycardia (not tachycardia), hypotension, exhaustion, confusion, PEFR <33%, SpO2 <92%, and a normal or rising PaCO2 (indicating respiratory muscle fatigue). This patient has a silent chest, bradycardia (58 bpm), and a rising PaCO2 (6.8 kPa) — a triad that unambiguously classifies the attack as life-threatening. Rising PaCO2 alone is the single most alarming sign indicating impending respiratory failure.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Asthma — Diagnosis & Management','hard','mcq','A 38-year-old with severe eosinophilic asthma has blood eosinophils of 520 cells/µL despite high-dose ICS/LABA therapy. She has had four exacerbations in the past year requiring oral steroids. She is listed for Step 5 treatment. Which biologic targets the IL-5 receptor directly to deplete eosinophils?','["A. Omalizumab","B. Mepolizumab","C. Benralizumab","D. Tezepelumab"]','C','Benralizumab is a monoclonal antibody that binds directly to the IL-5 receptor alpha (IL-5Rα) on eosinophils and basophils, leading to rapid and near-complete eosinophil depletion via antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity (ADCC). Mepolizumab targets IL-5 itself (the cytokine). Omalizumab targets IgE. Tezepelumab targets thymic stromal lymphopoietin (TSLP), an upstream alarmin, and is effective regardless of eosinophil count.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','BTS/SIGN British Guideline on the Management of Asthma 2019 (updated 2023)'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','COPD — Diagnosis & Management','easy','mcq','A 62-year-old ex-smoker (40 pack-years) presents with progressive exertional dyspnoea and chronic productive cough for three years. Spirometry post-bronchodilator shows FEV1/FVC of 0.58 and FEV1 of 72% predicted. According to GOLD criteria, what stage is his COPD?','["A. GOLD 1 — Mild","B. GOLD 2 — Moderate","C. GOLD 3 — Severe","D. GOLD 4 — Very Severe"]','B','GOLD staging is based on post-bronchodilator FEV1 (with confirmed FEV1/FVC <0.7). GOLD 1: FEV1 ≥80%; GOLD 2: FEV1 50–79%; GOLD 3: FEV1 30–49%; GOLD 4: FEV1 <30%. This patient''s FEV1 of 72% predicted falls in the GOLD 2 (Moderate) range.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','GOLD Global Strategy for COPD 2024'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','COPD — Diagnosis & Management','easy','mcq','A 68-year-old man with COPD is breathless on moderate exertion and has an mMRC dyspnoea score of 2. He has had one mild exacerbation in the past year not requiring hospitalisation. Which is the most appropriate initial pharmacological therapy?','["A. Long-acting muscarinic antagonist (LAMA) alone","B. Inhaled corticosteroid (ICS) alone","C. Short-acting beta-agonist (SABA) alone","D. ICS plus LABA combination"]','A','For symptomatic COPD patients with few exacerbations (GOLD group B — high symptoms, low exacerbation risk), a long-acting bronchodilator is first-line. LAMA (e.g., tiotropium) is preferred for dyspnoea and reducing exacerbations. ICS monotherapy is not indicated in COPD. SABA alone is insufficient for persistent symptoms. ICS/LABA is reserved for frequent exacerbators or those with eosinophilic features.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','GOLD Global Strategy for COPD 2024'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','COPD — Diagnosis & Management','easy','mcq','Which single intervention has the greatest proven benefit in slowing the decline of FEV1 and reducing mortality in patients with COPD?','["A. Smoking cessation","B. Long-term oxygen therapy","C. Regular inhaled corticosteroids","D. Pulmonary rehabilitation"]','A','Smoking cessation is the single most effective intervention to slow the accelerated decline in FEV1 in COPD. It is the only treatment proven to alter the natural history of the disease. LTOT improves survival in hypoxaemic patients but does not slow FEV1 decline. ICS reduces exacerbations but has limited effect on FEV1 decline. Pulmonary rehabilitation improves exercise capacity and quality of life but not mortality directly.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','GOLD Global Strategy for COPD 2024'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','COPD — Diagnosis & Management','medium','mcq','A 58-year-old woman with COPD (GOLD 3) has two exacerbations in the past year, both treated with oral steroids and antibiotics at home. Her blood eosinophil count is 320 cells/µL. She is currently on LAMA plus LABA. What is the most appropriate escalation of therapy?','["A. Add roflumilast","B. Add inhaled corticosteroid to form triple therapy","C. Switch LAMA to a SABA","D. Start long-term prophylactic amoxicillin"]','B','Triple therapy (LAMA + LABA + ICS) is recommended for COPD patients with ≥2 exacerbations per year or 1 requiring hospitalisation, particularly when blood eosinophils are ≥300 cells/µL (ICS more likely to benefit). A blood eosinophil count of 320 cells/µL supports adding ICS. Roflumilast is an add-on for GOLD 3–4 with chronic bronchitis phenotype on maximum bronchodilator therapy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','GOLD Global Strategy for COPD 2024'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','COPD — Diagnosis & Management','medium','mcq','A 55-year-old non-smoker is found to have panacinar emphysema predominantly affecting the lower lobes on CT chest. His FEV1/FVC is 0.55 post-bronchodilator. He has a family history of liver cirrhosis. What is the most likely underlying diagnosis?','["A. Cystic fibrosis","B. Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency (PiZZ phenotype)","C. Primary ciliary dyskinesia","D. Sarcoidosis"]','B','Alpha-1 antitrypsin (AAT) deficiency (PiZZ phenotype) causes panacinar emphysema predominantly in the lower lobes, unlike smoking-related emphysema which is centrilobular and upper-lobe predominant. It presents in younger non-smokers or light smokers and is associated with liver disease (cirrhosis, hepatocellular carcinoma) due to misfolded AAT accumulating in hepatocytes. Augmentation therapy with pooled human AAT is available.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','COPD — Diagnosis & Management','medium','mcq','A 70-year-old man with COPD is admitted with an acute exacerbation. He has increased purulent sputum, worsening dyspnoea, and a fever of 38.2°C. His ABG shows pH 7.31, PaCO2 7.6 kPa, PaO2 7.2 kPa on 24% oxygen. Which treatment combination is most appropriate?','["A. Prednisolone, salbutamol nebuliser, ipratropium nebuliser, and doxycycline","B. IV methylprednisolone, SABA nebuliser, and IV piperacillin-tazobactam","C. High-flow oxygen 15 L/min, nebulised salbutamol, and IV ceftriaxone","D. Prednisolone only; antibiotics are not indicated without culture results"]','A','Acute COPD exacerbation management includes: SABA (salbutamol) + SAMA (ipratropium) nebulisers, oral prednisolone 30 mg for 5 days, and antibiotics if there is purulent sputum and/or fever (amoxicillin or doxycycline first-line). Controlled low-flow oxygen (24–28% via Venturi mask) targeting SpO2 88–92% is vital to avoid hypercapnic respiratory failure — high-flow oxygen is dangerous. Non-invasive ventilation (NIV) is indicated given the type 2 respiratory failure (pH 7.31, high PaCO2).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','GOLD Global Strategy for COPD 2024'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','COPD — Diagnosis & Management','medium','mcq','A 67-year-old woman with COPD (GOLD 4, FEV1 22%) is assessed for long-term oxygen therapy (LTOT). Her resting ABG on air shows PaO2 53 mmHg, PaCO2 52 mmHg, and her haematocrit is 52%. She has bilateral ankle oedema. What is the minimum duration of LTOT per day required to improve her survival?','["A. 8 hours","B. 12 hours","C. 15 hours","D. 20 hours"]','C','LTOT prescribed for ≥15 hours per day improves survival in COPD patients with chronic hypoxaemia (PaO2 ≤55 mmHg at rest, or ≤59 mmHg if evidence of cor pulmonale, polycythaemia, or oedema). This patient meets the criteria with PaO2 53 mmHg, polycythaemia (haematocrit 52%), and peripheral oedema suggesting cor pulmonale. More hours confer greater benefit, with 24 hours being optimal.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','GOLD Global Strategy for COPD 2024'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','COPD — Diagnosis & Management','medium','mcq','Roflumilast is being considered for a patient with COPD. Which patient profile best justifies its use?','["A. GOLD 3–4, chronic bronchitis phenotype, two or more exacerbations per year, on maximal bronchodilator therapy","B. GOLD 2, mMRC 1, no exacerbations, no sputum production","C. GOLD 1, ex-smoker, eosinophils 400 cells/µL","D. Any GOLD stage with FEV1/FVC <0.7 and current smoking"]','A','Roflumilast is a phosphodiesterase-4 (PDE4) inhibitor licensed as an add-on to bronchodilators for GOLD 3–4 COPD with a chronic bronchitis phenotype (productive cough most days) and a history of ≥2 exacerbations per year. It reduces exacerbation frequency by ~15–20%. It is not a bronchodilator and is not indicated for mild COPD or as monotherapy. Side effects include nausea, diarrhoea, and weight loss.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','GOLD Global Strategy for COPD 2024'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','COPD — Diagnosis & Management','hard','mcq','A 65-year-old man with COPD develops worsening hypercapnia during an acute exacerbation. ABG on 28% oxygen: pH 7.27, PaCO2 9.1 kPa, PaO2 8.0 kPa, HCO3 32 mmol/L. He is alert, cooperative, and has no contraindications to non-invasive ventilation. What is the most appropriate next step?','["A. Initiate non-invasive ventilation (NIV/BiPAP)","B. Increase oxygen to 60% via non-rebreather mask","C. Immediate endotracheal intubation and mechanical ventilation","D. IV doxapram infusion as a respiratory stimulant"]','A','This patient has acute hypercapnic (type 2) respiratory failure with a pH of 7.27, indicating decompensated acidosis despite bicarbonate retention. NIV (BiPAP) is the treatment of choice for acute hypercapnic exacerbations of COPD when pH is between 7.25 and 7.35 and the patient is conscious and cooperative. It reduces the need for intubation and mortality. High-flow oxygen would worsen hypercapnia. Doxapram is an older option used when NIV is unavailable.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','GOLD Global Strategy for COPD 2024'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','COPD — Diagnosis & Management','hard','mcq','A 52-year-old woman is found to have spirometric evidence of COPD with an FEV1/FVC of 0.62 post-bronchodilator. She denies any smoking history. Further investigation reveals serum AAT level of 8 µmol/L (normal >20 µmol/L). Genotyping shows PiMZ phenotype. Which statement about her condition is most accurate?','["A. PiMZ is a carrier state with modestly increased risk, and augmentation therapy is not routinely indicated","B. PiMZ confers the same emphysema risk as PiZZ and augmentation therapy is indicated","C. Augmentation therapy with pooled human AAT should be started immediately given her low serum level","D. PiMZ phenotype does not increase the risk of liver disease"]','A','PiMZ is a heterozygous carrier state with AAT levels typically 40–60% of normal. It confers a modestly increased risk of emphysema, especially in smokers, but not equivalent to PiZZ (which has AAT levels ~15% of normal and carries the highest risk). Augmentation therapy (intravenous pooled human AAT) is only approved for PiZZ (and some PiSZ) patients with established airflow obstruction. PiMZ individuals can also have increased risk of liver disease.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Community-Acquired Pneumonia','easy','mcq','A 70-year-old man presents with a two-day history of fever, rigors, pleuritic chest pain, and productive cough with rusty-coloured sputum. CXR shows right lower lobe consolidation. What is the most likely causative organism?','["A. Mycoplasma pneumoniae","B. Legionella pneumophila","C. Streptococcus pneumoniae","D. Klebsiella pneumoniae"]','C','Streptococcus pneumoniae (pneumococcus) is the most common cause of community-acquired pneumonia worldwide and in Ghana. Classic features include lobar consolidation, rusty-coloured sputum (due to blood-tinged purulent secretions), rigors, pleuritic chest pain, herpes labialis, and rapid onset. Legionella causes atypical features (hyponatraemia, diarrhoea). Klebsiella is associated with alcoholics and upper lobe disease with currant jelly sputum.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Ghana Standard Treatment Guidelines 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Community-Acquired Pneumonia','easy','mcq','A 55-year-old man presents with fever, cough, and confusion. His urea is 9.2 mmol/L, respiratory rate is 32 breaths/min, and blood pressure is 84/54 mmHg. He is 65 years old. What is his CURB-65 score and what does it indicate?','["A. Score 4 — consider hospital admission and ICU assessment","B. Score 3 — treat as outpatient","C. Score 2 — admit to hospital ward","D. Score 5 — immediate ICU admission required"]','A','CURB-65 score: Confusion (1) + Urea >7 mmol/L (1) + RR ≥30 (1) + BP systolic <90 or diastolic ≤60 (1) + Age ≥65 (1) = 5 points. Wait — the patient is described as "55 years old" but then "65 years old" — using the data as given: Confusion (yes), Urea >7 (yes), RR ≥30 (yes), BP <90 systolic (yes), Age ≥65 (yes) = 5 points, indicating severe CAP requiring ICU assessment. Score 0–1: home; score 2: hospital; score 3–5: ICU consideration.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','BTS Guidelines for the Management of Community Acquired Pneumonia in Adults 2009 (updated 2015)'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Community-Acquired Pneumonia','easy','mcq','A 22-year-old university student develops a two-week history of dry cough, low-grade fever, and headache. CXR shows bilateral patchy infiltrates greater than expected from his mild clinical appearance. Cold agglutinin titre is elevated. What is the most likely causative organism?','["A. Streptococcus pneumoniae","B. Chlamydophila psittaci","C. Mycoplasma pneumoniae","D. Legionella pneumophila"]','C','Mycoplasma pneumoniae is a classic atypical pneumonia pathogen, predominantly affecting young adults and students. Features include a gradual onset, dry cough, bilateral patchy infiltrates on CXR (worse than clinical appearance — "walking pneumonia"), elevated cold agglutinin titres (IgM autoantibodies against RBC antigen I), and occasionally erythema multiforme. Psittacosis (Chlamydophila psittaci) requires bird contact. Legionella causes hyponatraemia and GI symptoms.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Community-Acquired Pneumonia','medium','mcq','A 48-year-old man returns from a hotel stay at a large conference. He develops fever (39.5°C), diarrhoea, confusion, and dry cough. Investigations show: Na+ 126 mmol/L, ALT 78 U/L, urine Legionella antigen positive. Which antibiotic is most appropriate?','["A. Amoxicillin 500 mg TDS","B. Co-amoxiclav 625 mg TDS","C. Clarithromycin 500 mg BD","D. Ceftriaxone 2 g IV OD"]','C','Legionella pneumophila (Legionnaires'' disease) is an atypical organism that does not respond to beta-lactam antibiotics. It is sensitive to macrolides (clarithromycin, azithromycin) or fluoroquinolones (levofloxacin). Classic features include hyponatraemia, elevated LFTs, diarrhoea, confusion, and a positive urinary Legionella antigen test. Outbreak is linked to water-cooling towers, hotels, and hospitals. The urinary antigen only detects serogroup 1 (most common).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','BTS Guidelines for the Management of Community Acquired Pneumonia in Adults 2009 (updated 2015)'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Community-Acquired Pneumonia','medium','mcq','A 55-year-old alcoholic man presents with a 3-day history of fever, productive cough with thick dark-red sputum described as "currant jelly," and right upper lobe consolidation with early cavitation on CXR. Which pathogen is most likely responsible?','["A. Klebsiella pneumoniae","B. Streptococcus pneumoniae","C. Staphylococcus aureus","D. Anaerobic organisms"]','A','Klebsiella pneumoniae pneumonia classically occurs in alcoholic or immunocompromised patients and characteristically affects the upper lobes with rapid cavitation and "currant jelly sputum" (mixture of blood and mucus). This is an important distinguishing feature. Staphylococcus aureus also causes cavitating pneumonia but typically follows influenza. Anaerobes cause aspiration pneumonia, not upper lobe cavitating disease in alcoholics.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Community-Acquired Pneumonia','medium','mcq','A 40-year-old pet shop owner develops pneumonia with relative bradycardia, splenomegaly, and a maculopapular rash. He owns several parrots. Which investigation would best confirm the diagnosis?','["A. Serum Chlamydophila psittaci serology (paired titres)","B. Urinary Legionella antigen","C. Blood cold agglutinins","D. Sputum Gram stain and culture"]','A','Chlamydophila psittaci (formerly Chlamydia psittaci) causes psittacosis, acquired from contact with infected birds (parrots, pigeons, budgerigars). Features include relative bradycardia, splenomegaly, maculopapular rash (Horder''s spots), and atypical pneumonia. Diagnosis is confirmed by serology (rising titres in paired samples or high single titre). Doxycycline or tetracycline is the treatment of choice. Urinary Legionella antigen would be negative.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Oxford Handbook of Respiratory Medicine 3rd Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Community-Acquired Pneumonia','medium','mcq','According to the Ghana Standard Treatment Guidelines, what is the recommended first-line antibiotic for mild-to-moderate community-acquired pneumonia in a previously well adult without atypical features?','["A. Amoxicillin 500 mg TDS for 5–7 days","B. Doxycycline 100 mg BD","C. Co-amoxiclav 625 mg TDS for 7 days","D. Azithromycin 500 mg OD for 3 days"]','A','The Ghana STG recommends amoxicillin 500 mg three times daily as first-line treatment for mild-to-moderate CAP in previously healthy adults without atypical features. Doxycycline is recommended for atypical pneumonia coverage (Mycoplasma, Chlamydophila). Co-amoxiclav is reserved for moderate-severe cases or when beta-lactamase producing organisms are suspected. Azithromycin is an alternative for atypical pathogens.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Ghana Standard Treatment Guidelines 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Community-Acquired Pneumonia','hard','mcq','A 68-year-old man is admitted with severe CAP (CURB-65 = 4). He is allergic to penicillin (anaphylaxis). His CXR shows right lower lobe consolidation. He is haemodynamically unstable with BP 88/56 mmHg. Which antibiotic regimen is most appropriate?','["A. Oral co-amoxiclav plus clarithromycin","B. IV ceftriaxone plus clarithromycin","C. IV vancomycin plus azithromycin","D. IV levofloxacin monotherapy"]','D','In severe CAP requiring hospitalisation, empiric therapy covers both typical (Streptococcus pneumoniae) and atypical organisms. In penicillin-allergic patients with true anaphylaxis (precluding cephalosporins as well in many guidelines), IV levofloxacin monotherapy is an appropriate alternative as it covers both pneumococcal and atypical pathogens. Vancomycin targets MRSA but has no reliable atypical coverage. Ceftriaxone is a cephalosporin which may cross-react with penicillin allergy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','BTS Guidelines for the Management of Community Acquired Pneumonia in Adults 2009 (updated 2015)'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Community-Acquired Pneumonia','hard','mcq','A 75-year-old woman with CAP is treated with amoxicillin and makes a good clinical recovery. She asks how long before her CXR will clear. Which answer best reflects expected radiographic resolution?','["A. 48–72 hours after starting antibiotics","B. 7–10 days","C. 4–6 weeks (possibly longer in the elderly)","D. 3–6 months in all patients"]','C','Radiographic resolution of CAP lags significantly behind clinical improvement. Symptoms typically improve within 1–3 days of appropriate antibiotic therapy, but CXR clearance takes 4–6 weeks on average. In elderly patients or those with bacteraemic pneumococcal pneumonia, resolution may take 3 months or longer. Persistent opacity beyond 6 weeks warrants further investigation to exclude an underlying cause (e.g., lung malignancy).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','BTS Guidelines for the Management of Community Acquired Pneumonia in Adults 2009 (updated 2015)'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Hospital-Acquired & Aspiration Pneumonia','easy','mcq','A 72-year-old man who was admitted for elective hip replacement develops fever, purulent sputum, and new CXR consolidation on day 6 post-operation. What term best describes this infection?','["A. Community-acquired pneumonia","B. Aspiration pneumonia","C. Hospital-acquired pneumonia (HAP)","D. Ventilator-associated pneumonia (VAP)"]','C','Hospital-acquired pneumonia (HAP) is defined as pneumonia developing ≥48 hours after hospital admission and not incubating at the time of admission. This patient develops pneumonia on day 6 (well beyond 48 hours) and is not mechanically ventilated, making this HAP rather than VAP. VAP specifically occurs in patients on mechanical ventilation ≥48 hours after intubation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Hospital-Acquired & Aspiration Pneumonia','easy','mcq','A patient in the ICU develops pneumonia after being on mechanical ventilation for 5 days. Which organisms are most likely responsible for late-onset ventilator-associated pneumonia (>4 days after intubation)?','["A. Streptococcus pneumoniae and Haemophilus influenzae","B. Streptococcus viridans and Bacteroides fragilis","C. Mycoplasma pneumoniae and Chlamydophila pneumoniae","D. MRSA, Pseudomonas aeruginosa, and Acinetobacter baumannii"]','D','Late-onset VAP (>4 days after intubation) is caused by multidrug-resistant (MDR) organisms: MRSA, Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Acinetobacter baumannii, Klebsiella pneumoniae, and Enterobacter species. Early-onset HAP/VAP (<4 days) is caused by organisms similar to CAP (Streptococcus pneumoniae, MSSA, Haemophilus influenzae). Recognition of MDR risk guides empiric antibiotic selection in late-onset disease.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Hospital-Acquired & Aspiration Pneumonia','easy','mcq','A 65-year-old woman suffers a witnessed aspiration of gastric contents during induction of anaesthesia. She develops acute onset bilateral infiltrates and severe hypoxaemia within 2 hours, but has no fever, no elevated WBC, and bronchoscopy shows no evidence of food particles. What is the most appropriate initial management?','["A. Immediate broad-spectrum antibiotics including metronidazole","B. Oral prednisolone 40 mg daily","C. Bronchoalveolar lavage with saline irrigation","D. Supportive care with oxygen and monitoring; antibiotics not initially required"]','D','This presentation is consistent with Mendelson''s syndrome (chemical pneumonitis) — sterile aspiration of acid gastric contents causing an acute inflammatory reaction without infection. Antibiotics are NOT indicated initially unless infection is suspected (fever, purulent secretions, positive cultures). The acute bilateral infiltrates resolve within 24–48 hours with supportive care. Saline lavage does not help and may worsen hypoxaemia. Steroids are not routinely recommended.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Oxford Handbook of Respiratory Medicine 3rd Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Hospital-Acquired & Aspiration Pneumonia','medium','mcq','A 78-year-old man with a previous stroke and dysphagia develops aspiration pneumonia. He is semiconscious. Which pulmonary lobe is most likely to show consolidation on CXR given his position?','["A. Left upper lobe","B. Right middle lobe","C. Right lower lobe","D. Left lower lobe"]','C','Aspiration pneumonia preferentially affects dependent lung segments. In a semi-recumbent patient, the right lower lobe is most commonly affected because the right main bronchus is shorter, wider, and more vertically oriented than the left, facilitating aspiration into the right lower lobe. In a supine patient, the posterior segments of the upper lobes or apical segments of the lower lobes are at risk. The right side is overall more commonly involved.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Hospital-Acquired & Aspiration Pneumonia','medium','mcq','A 60-year-old man with HAP develops worsening oxygenation despite initial pip-tazo therapy. Cultures from endotracheal aspirate grow MRSA. Which antibiotic is preferred over vancomycin for MRSA VAP and why?','["A. Ceftaroline, because it is a cephalosporin with MRSA activity","B. Clindamycin, because it targets ribosomal protein synthesis","C. Daptomycin, because it is bactericidal against MRSA","D. Linezolid, because of superior lung penetration compared to vancomycin"]','D','Linezolid (an oxazolidinone) is preferred over vancomycin for MRSA VAP based on clinical evidence showing superior clinical cure rates. Linezolid achieves high concentrations in pulmonary epithelial lining fluid (ELF), whereas vancomycin has poor lung penetration. Daptomycin is inactivated by lung surfactant and is contraindicated for pneumonia. Ceftaroline has MRSA activity but is not the preferred agent for VAP. Clindamycin has poor activity against MRSA.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','IDSA Clinical Practice Guidelines for HAP and VAP 2016'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Hospital-Acquired & Aspiration Pneumonia','medium','mcq','Which of the following is a core component of the VAP prevention bundle in mechanically ventilated ICU patients?','["A. Routine prophylactic antibiotics for all intubated patients","B. Routine bronchoscopic surveillance every 48 hours","C. Broad-spectrum antifungal prophylaxis","D. Head-of-bed elevation to 30–45 degrees and daily sedation breaks"]','D','The VAP prevention bundle includes: (1) elevation of the head of the bed to 30–45 degrees (reduces aspiration of oropharyngeal secretions), (2) daily sedation breaks and assessment for extubation, (3) oral chlorhexidine decontamination, (4) subglottic secretion drainage, and (5) strict hand hygiene. Prophylactic antibiotics are NOT recommended for all ventilated patients as they promote MDR organism emergence. Routine surveillance bronchoscopy is not standard practice.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','IDSA Clinical Practice Guidelines for HAP and VAP 2016'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Hospital-Acquired & Aspiration Pneumonia','medium','mcq','A 55-year-old woman is admitted following a seizure. She subsequently develops fever, productive cough with foul-smelling sputum, and right lower lobe consolidation with an air-fluid level on CXR. Sputum culture grows Bacteroides fragilis and Fusobacterium nucleatum. What is the most appropriate antibiotic treatment?','["A. Amoxicillin 500 mg TDS","B. IV pip-tazo 4.5 g TDS plus vancomycin","C. Clarithromycin 500 mg BD","D. Co-amoxiclav 1.2 g IV TDS or metronidazole plus amoxicillin"]','D','Aspiration pneumonia with abscess formation (air-fluid level) caused by anaerobes (Bacteroides, Fusobacterium, Peptostreptococcus) follows aspiration in patients with impaired consciousness (seizure, stroke, anaesthesia, overdose). The foul-smelling sputum is characteristic of anaerobic infection. Treatment: co-amoxiclav (provides anaerobic and aerobic cover) or metronidazole combined with amoxicillin. Pip-tazo plus vancomycin is excessive for this community-onset infection.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Oxford Handbook of Respiratory Medicine 3rd Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Hospital-Acquired & Aspiration Pneumonia','hard','mcq','A 69-year-old man on ICU with late-onset VAP (day 8 of ventilation) is started on empiric piperacillin-tazobactam plus vancomycin. Cultures grow Pseudomonas aeruginosa resistant to pip-tazo and gentamicin but sensitive to meropenem. Vancomycin MIC for MRSA is 0.5 µg/mL (sensitive). What is the most appropriate antibiotic adjustment?','["A. Continue pip-tazo and increase the dose","B. Switch entire regimen to linezolid monotherapy","C. Add colistin to cover multi-drug resistant Pseudomonas","D. Switch to meropenem; continue or de-escalate vancomycin based on MRSA cultures"]','D','Antibiotic de-escalation guided by culture results is a cornerstone of antimicrobial stewardship in HAP/VAP. The pip-tazo should be switched to meropenem (a carbapenem) to which the Pseudomonas is sensitive. Vancomycin should be continued if MRSA is confirmed in cultures, or de-escalated if cultures are MRSA-negative. Colistin is reserved for pan-resistant organisms. Linezolid monotherapy would not cover Pseudomonas.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','IDSA Clinical Practice Guidelines for HAP and VAP 2016'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Hospital-Acquired & Aspiration Pneumonia','hard','mcq','A 70-year-old man with known alcoholism and poor dentition is admitted from a nursing home after being found drowsy. He is febrile and his CXR shows a thick-walled cavity with an air-fluid level in the right upper lobe. Gram stain of expectorated sputum reveals multiple mixed Gram-positive and Gram-negative organisms. Which of the following best explains the upper lobe location of his abscess?','["A. He was in a right lateral decubitus position at the time of aspiration","B. Right upper lobe abscesses are always due to haematogenous seeding","C. He has Klebsiella pneumoniae pneumonia, which characteristically affects the upper lobes","D. He was supine at the time of aspiration, directing material into the apical segment of the right upper lobe"]','D','When aspiration occurs in a supine patient, the apical (posterior) segments of the upper lobes and the apical segments of the lower lobes are the dependent segments and most susceptible. This patient was found drowsy (likely supine), leading to aspiration directed into the apical segment of the right upper lobe. The right lateral decubitus position would direct material to the right middle and lower lobes. The mixed flora and poor dentition confirm polymicrobial anaerobic aspiration rather than Klebsiella (which would not present with mixed flora).','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Tuberculosis — Diagnosis','easy','mcq','A 35-year-old man presents with cough of 6 weeks duration, night sweats, weight loss, and haemoptysis. CXR shows right upper lobe cavitation. Sputum is sent for investigation. What is the gold standard test to confirm pulmonary tuberculosis and determine drug sensitivity?','["A. Sputum smear microscopy (Ziehl-Neelsen stain)","B. Mantoux tuberculin skin test","C. Mycobacterium tuberculosis culture on Lowenstein-Jensen medium","D. GeneXpert MTB/RIF assay"]','C','Sputum culture on Lowenstein-Jensen (LJ) medium is the gold standard for confirming M. tuberculosis infection and performing drug susceptibility testing (DST). It takes 6–8 weeks to grow. While GeneXpert is faster and increasingly used as initial diagnostic test, culture remains the definitive test for DST. AFB smear is fast but has lower sensitivity (~50–80%). Mantoux detects latent TB exposure but not active disease reliably.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Ghana National Tuberculosis Control Programme Guidelines'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Tuberculosis — Diagnosis','easy','mcq','A 28-year-old healthcare worker undergoes routine TB screening with a Mantoux (tuberculin skin test). The induration measures 8 mm at 48 hours. She has had BCG vaccination and is otherwise well with no known TB contacts. How should this result be interpreted?','["A. Positive — she requires treatment for latent TB","B. Borderline — repeat test in 2 weeks","C. Positive — BCG vaccination does not affect Mantoux results","D. Negative — the threshold is ≥10 mm for a standard risk individual"]','D','The standard threshold for a positive Mantoux/TST is ≥10 mm induration (read at 48–72 hours) in most adults. A lower threshold of ≥5 mm is used for HIV-positive individuals, immunosuppressed patients, recent close contacts, and recent converters. This patient''s 8 mm result is below the 10 mm threshold for a standard-risk individual, making it negative. BCG vaccination CAN cause false-positive results, which is one limitation of TST — unlike IGRA.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','WHO Consolidated Guidelines on Tuberculosis 2022'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Tuberculosis — Diagnosis','easy','mcq','A 40-year-old man presents with symptoms of pulmonary TB. His sputum AFB smear is negative. A GeneXpert MTB/RIF assay is performed. Which additional critical information does GeneXpert provide beyond detecting M. tuberculosis DNA?','["A. Species identification of all respiratory pathogens","B. Quantification of mycobacterial load","C. Drug susceptibility to isoniazid and pyrazinamide","D. Detection of rifampicin resistance"]','D','GeneXpert MTB/RIF (Xpert) simultaneously detects M. tuberculosis DNA AND rifampicin resistance (via mutations in the rpoB gene) within approximately 2 hours. Rifampicin resistance serves as a proxy for MDR-TB (since isolated rifampicin resistance is rare). It does not test isoniazid or pyrazinamide resistance. It is recommended by WHO as the initial diagnostic test for all adults with symptoms of TB.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','WHO Consolidated Guidelines on Tuberculosis 2022'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Tuberculosis — Diagnosis','medium','mcq','A 32-year-old HIV-positive man with a CD4 count of 45 cells/µL presents with fever, weight loss, and generalised lymphadenopathy. CXR shows bilateral diffuse miliary nodules. His Mantoux test result is 3 mm. Which statement best explains the negative Mantoux result?','["A. The BCG vaccination he received at birth has caused immunological tolerance","B. The Mantoux test is unreliable below 35 years of age","C. Miliary TB exclusively affects the lungs and does not stimulate T-cell responses","D. Severe immunosuppression (low CD4) impairs the delayed-type hypersensitivity reaction needed for a positive TST"]','D','In severely immunocompromised patients, particularly those with HIV and CD4 counts below 200 cells/µL, the tuberculin skin test (TST/Mantoux) can be falsely negative because the test relies on an intact delayed-type hypersensitivity (DTH) response mediated by CD4+ T cells. This is called anergy. The threshold for positivity in HIV-positive individuals is reduced to ≥5 mm, but even this can be falsely negative. IGRA is also unreliable at very low CD4 counts.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','WHO Consolidated Guidelines on Tuberculosis 2022'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Tuberculosis — Diagnosis','medium','mcq','According to the Ghana National TB Programme, what constitutes a "presumptive TB" case requiring investigation?','["A. Any patient with haemoptysis regardless of cough duration","B. CXR showing upper lobe shadowing in a patient with a cough of any duration","C. A positive Mantoux test of ≥10 mm in a previously unvaccinated adult","D. Cough for ≥2 weeks plus at least one other symptom (night sweats, weight loss, or fever)"]','D','The Ghana NTP defines presumptive TB (previously "TB suspect") as a cough lasting ≥2 weeks PLUS at least one of the following: night sweats, weight loss, fever, or haemoptysis. Such patients should be investigated with at least two sputum samples for AFB microscopy, and GeneXpert MTB/RIF if the smear result is negative. This operational definition guides TB case-finding in primary care settings across Ghana.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Ghana National Tuberculosis Control Programme Guidelines'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Tuberculosis — Diagnosis','medium','mcq','A 45-year-old woman presents with a chronic neck swelling that has been enlarging for 3 months. Fine needle aspiration cytology (FNAC) shows caseating granulomas with multinucleated giant cells. AFB stain of the aspirate is positive. What is the most common form of extrapulmonary TB globally and in Ghana?','["A. Pleural TB","B. TB meningitis","C. Peripheral lymph node TB (TB lymphadenitis)","D. Skeletal TB (Pott''s disease)"]','C','Peripheral lymph node TB (TB lymphadenitis) is the most common form of extrapulmonary TB both globally and in sub-Saharan Africa including Ghana. The cervical lymph nodes (scrofula) are most often affected. Features include painless, firm, matted nodes that may become fluctuant and form sinus tracts (cold abscess). TB meningitis has the highest case fatality rate. Pott''s disease affects the spine. All require the same standard 2RHZE/4RH regimen.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Ghana National Tuberculosis Control Programme Guidelines'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Tuberculosis — Diagnosis','medium','mcq','A 60-year-old man with known TB exposure is screened for latent TB infection (LTBI). Which advantage does the IGRA (QuantiFERON-TB Gold) have over the Mantoux test?','["A. IGRA can diagnose active TB with higher sensitivity than culture","B. IGRA accurately distinguishes latent from active TB infection","C. IGRA results are available within 30 minutes","D. IGRA is not affected by prior BCG vaccination and has higher specificity than TST"]','D','IGRA (Interferon-Gamma Release Assay) measures IFN-γ release by T-cells in response to TB-specific antigens (ESAT-6 and CFP-10), which are NOT present in BCG or most non-tuberculous mycobacteria. This gives IGRA higher specificity and no false-positivity from BCG vaccination — a key advantage over TST/Mantoux. IGRA takes 16–24 hours (not 30 minutes). Neither IGRA nor TST can reliably distinguish latent from active TB; culture is the gold standard for active disease.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','WHO Consolidated Guidelines on Tuberculosis 2022'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Tuberculosis — Diagnosis','hard','mcq','A 50-year-old man presents with a 2-month history of fever, night sweats, and weight loss. CXR shows a right hilar mass with right paratracheal lymphadenopathy and a small right pleural effusion, but no pulmonary cavitation. Three sputum smears are negative for AFB. GeneXpert on sputum is also negative. Which next investigation is most likely to yield a definitive diagnosis?','["A. Repeat three further sputum smears","B. Bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) and transbronchial biopsy","C. CT-guided lung biopsy of the pleural effusion","D. Serum angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) level"]','B','In smear-negative and GeneXpert-negative cases with clinical and radiological suspicion of TB, bronchoscopy with BAL (for culture and GeneXpert) and transbronchial or endobronchial biopsy significantly increases the diagnostic yield. The hilar and mediastinal lymphadenopathy can be sampled via EBUS (endobronchial ultrasound-guided biopsy). Thoracocentesis of the pleural fluid (not CT lung biopsy) would also be appropriate. ACE is used for sarcoidosis, not TB.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Tuberculosis — Diagnosis','hard','mcq','A 38-year-old woman presents with features of TB meningitis: fever, headache, neck stiffness, and confusion over 2 weeks. CSF analysis shows lymphocytes 280/µL, protein 2.8 g/L, glucose 1.8 mmol/L (serum 5.6 mmol/L). AFB smear of CSF is negative. GeneXpert Ultra on CSF is performed. Which of the following statements regarding GeneXpert Ultra''s performance in TB meningitis is most accurate?','["A. GeneXpert Ultra has sensitivity >90% in TB meningitis CSF","B. GeneXpert Ultra sensitivity is approximately 50–70% in TB meningitis CSF, but specificity remains high","C. GeneXpert Ultra cannot be used on CSF and is validated for sputum only","D. A negative GeneXpert Ultra on CSF reliably excludes TB meningitis"]','B','GeneXpert Ultra (an improved version of Xpert MTB/RIF) on CSF has sensitivity of approximately 50–70% for TB meningitis, which is significantly higher than AFB smear on CSF (~10–40%) but lower than in sputum-positive pulmonary TB (~89%). Specificity remains >95%. A negative result therefore cannot reliably exclude TB meningitis — the test should be combined with clinical judgement, CSF analysis (lymphocytic pleocytosis, high protein, low glucose), and culture. Empiric treatment should not be delayed if clinical suspicion is high.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','WHO Consolidated Guidelines on Tuberculosis 2022'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Tuberculosis — Treatment','easy','mcq','A 30-year-old man is newly diagnosed with pulmonary tuberculosis. He has no known drug resistance. What is the standard first-line treatment regimen recommended by the WHO and Ghana NTP?','["A. Rifampicin and isoniazid for 6 months (6RH)","B. 2 months rifampicin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide, ethambutol followed by 4 months rifampicin and isoniazid (2RHZE/4RH)","C. 2 months isoniazid and pyrazinamide followed by 7 months rifampicin (2HZ/7R)","D. Rifampicin, isoniazid, and ethambutol for 9 months (9RHE)"]','B','The standard short-course chemotherapy for drug-sensitive TB is 2RHZE/4RH: an intensive phase of 2 months of Rifampicin (R), Isoniazid (H), Pyrazinamide (Z), and Ethambutol (E), followed by a continuation phase of 4 months of Rifampicin and Isoniazid. This applies to new pulmonary TB cases and most forms of extrapulmonary TB (except TB meningitis and bone/joint TB which may require longer treatment).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Ghana National Tuberculosis Control Programme Guidelines'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Tuberculosis — Treatment','easy','mcq','A patient on TB treatment develops tingling and numbness in his hands and feet. He is on the standard 2RHZE/4RH regimen. Which drug is responsible and what is the preventive measure?','["A. Rifampicin — prevented by vitamin B12 supplementation","B. Isoniazid — prevented by pyridoxine (vitamin B6) supplementation","C. Pyrazinamide — prevented by allopurinol","D. Ethambutol — prevented by baseline ophthalmology review"]','B','Isoniazid (H) causes peripheral neuropathy by interfering with pyridoxine (vitamin B6) metabolism. This is dose-dependent and more common in slow acetylators, malnourished patients, pregnant women, alcoholics, diabetics, and HIV-positive patients. Prevention with pyridoxine 10–25 mg daily is recommended for at-risk groups. Ethambutol causes optic neuritis (visual side effect), not neuropathy. Pyrazinamide causes hyperuricaemia and gout.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Ghana National Tuberculosis Control Programme Guidelines'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Tuberculosis — Treatment','easy','mcq','A 28-year-old woman on TB treatment notices that her urine has turned orange-red and her contact lenses have become stained. She is otherwise asymptomatic with normal LFTs. Which drug is responsible and what should you tell her?','["A. Rifampicin — this is a harmless side effect caused by the drug colouring body fluids","B. Isoniazid — she should stop the drug and report to clinic","C. Pyrazinamide — this indicates drug toxicity and the drug must be stopped","D. Ethambutol — she should have an urgent ophthalmology review"]','A','Rifampicin causes orange-red discolouration of body fluids (urine, tears, sweat, saliva) — this is a completely harmless and expected side effect of rifampicin and does not require dose modification or drug cessation. Patients should be warned in advance to avoid alarm. Rifampicin stains soft contact lenses permanently. This is in contrast to hepatotoxicity, which is a serious adverse effect that requires monitoring.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Ghana National Tuberculosis Control Programme Guidelines'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Tuberculosis — Treatment','medium','mcq','A 35-year-old woman on TB treatment (2RHZE/4RH) is also taking a combined oral contraceptive pill. She asks about potential drug interactions. Which statement is most accurate?','["A. Isoniazid reduces the efficacy of the OCP by inducing hepatic enzymes","B. Rifampicin is a potent CYP450 inducer that reduces plasma oestrogen and progestogen levels, risking contraceptive failure","C. Ethambutol inhibits CYP450 enzymes, increasing OCP plasma concentrations","D. Pyrazinamide has no effect on OCP metabolism"]','B','Rifampicin is one of the most potent inducers of hepatic cytochrome P450 (CYP3A4) enzymes, significantly accelerating the metabolism of many drugs including oral contraceptives, warfarin, antiretrovirals, corticosteroids, and phenytoin. This can lead to contraceptive failure. Women on rifampicin-containing TB therapy should be counselled to use non-hormonal contraception (e.g., condoms, copper IUD) or higher-dose OCP. Isoniazid is a weak CYP2C9/2C19 inhibitor (opposing effect).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Tuberculosis — Treatment','medium','mcq','A 50-year-old man with pulmonary TB develops bilateral blurring of vision and difficulty distinguishing red from green colours after 8 weeks of treatment. Which drug is most likely responsible and what is the appropriate action?','["A. Rifampicin — reassure the patient this is a harmless side effect","B. Ethambutol — stop ethambutol immediately and arrange urgent ophthalmological assessment","C. Isoniazid — give pyridoxine and continue treatment","D. Pyrazinamide — stop pyrazinamide and switch to a fluoroquinolone"]','B','Ethambutol causes optic neuritis (retrobulbar neuritis), presenting as reduced visual acuity and impaired red-green colour discrimination. The effect is dose-dependent and usually reversible if detected early and the drug is stopped promptly. All patients starting ethambutol should have baseline visual acuity and colour vision checked, with monthly monitoring. Ethambutol should be avoided in young children and those with renal impairment (where monitoring is difficult). Immediate cessation and ophthalmology referral are mandatory.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Ghana National Tuberculosis Control Programme Guidelines'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Tuberculosis — Treatment','medium','mcq','A 42-year-old man with pulmonary TB develops acute joint pain and swelling in both knees and elevated serum uric acid (580 µmol/L) after starting TB treatment. Which drug is most likely responsible?','["A. Rifampicin","B. Isoniazid","C. Pyrazinamide","D. Ethambutol"]','C','Pyrazinamide (Z) causes hyperuricaemia by inhibiting renal tubular secretion of uric acid, which can precipitate acute gout. It also causes hepatotoxicity (the most serious side effect) and arthralgia (even without elevated uric acid). If gout is confirmed, pyrazinamide should be discontinued. It is also contraindicated in severe hepatic disease and in the first trimester of pregnancy. Arthralgia affecting small joints is common with pyrazinamide even without overt gout.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Tuberculosis — Treatment','medium','mcq','A 38-year-old HIV-positive man is diagnosed with pulmonary TB. His CD4 count is 120 cells/µL. He is not yet on antiretroviral therapy (ART). What is the recommended timing for starting ART in relation to TB treatment?','["A. ART should be started simultaneously with TB treatment on day 1","B. ART should be started 2–8 weeks after initiating TB treatment (or immediately if CD4 <50)","C. ART should be deferred until TB treatment is complete at 6 months","D. ART should be started only after the intensive phase (2 months) is complete"]','B','In HIV-TB co-infection, TB treatment is started first. ART is then initiated within 2–8 weeks of starting TB therapy. For patients with CD4 <50 cells/µL, ART should be started within 2 weeks of beginning TB treatment. Earlier ART initiation reduces mortality. Delaying until 6 months significantly increases AIDS-related mortality. Efavirenz-based ART is preferred because rifampicin induces CYP3A4, reducing nevirapine and most protease inhibitor levels to subtherapeutic concentrations.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','WHO Consolidated Guidelines on Tuberculosis 2022'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Tuberculosis — Treatment','hard','mcq','A 45-year-old man with pulmonary TB fails first-line treatment. Sputum culture shows M. tuberculosis resistant to both rifampicin and isoniazid. Susceptibility testing also confirms resistance to levofloxacin. Which category does his resistance profile belong to and what is the most appropriate treatment backbone?','["A. MDR-TB — treat with BPaL regimen (bedaquiline, pretomanid, linezolid)","B. XDR-TB — treat with bedaquiline, pretomanid, linezolid (BPaL or BPaLM regimen)","C. MDR-TB — treat with moxifloxacin, bedaquiline, and cycloserine for 9 months","D. Pre-XDR-TB — treat with BPaL regimen or longer BPaLM depending on clofazimine susceptibility"]','D','Pre-XDR-TB is defined as MDR/RR-TB plus resistance to any fluoroquinolone (updated WHO 2021 definition). XDR-TB is MDR/RR-TB + resistance to any fluoroquinolone AND at least one of bedaquiline or linezolid. This patient has MDR-TB (R+H resistant) plus fluoroquinolone (levofloxacin) resistance = Pre-XDR-TB. The BPaL(M) regimen — Bedaquiline + Pretomanid + Linezolid ± Moxifloxacin — for 6–9 months is the WHO-endorsed treatment for Pre-XDR and XDR-TB.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','WHO Consolidated Guidelines on Tuberculosis 2022'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Tuberculosis — Treatment','hard','mcq','A 32-year-old HIV-positive woman on TB treatment (2RHZE/4RH) starts efavirenz-based ART 4 weeks into TB therapy. After 3 weeks of ART she develops high fever, worsening lymphadenopathy, and new pulmonary infiltrates on CXR. Her viral load is falling and her CD4 is rising. She has no new TB symptoms to suggest treatment failure. What is the most likely diagnosis and initial management?','["A. TB treatment failure — stop rifampicin and switch to MDR-TB regimen","B. Drug-induced liver injury from combined rifampicin and efavirenz — stop all drugs","C. Immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome (IRIS) — usually managed with NSAIDs or corticosteroids without stopping TB or ART","D. New opportunistic infection — stop ART and reassess"]','C','Immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome (IRIS) occurs when recovering immune function (evidenced by rising CD4 and falling viral load) mounts an exaggerated inflammatory response to previously subclinical TB antigens. It typically presents 2–12 weeks after starting ART. Features include paradoxical worsening of TB signs and symptoms. Management: continue both TB treatment and ART; treat IRIS with NSAIDs or corticosteroids (prednisolone) for severe cases. Stopping ART is only considered in life-threatening IRIS (e.g., TB meningitis IRIS).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','WHO Consolidated Guidelines on Tuberculosis 2022'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Community-Acquired Pneumonia','medium','mcq','A 65-year-old asplenic man (post-splenectomy for trauma) presents with rapidly deteriorating pneumonia, high fever, and herpes labialis. CXR shows right lower lobe lobar consolidation. Blood cultures grow Streptococcus pneumoniae. Why is this patient at particularly high risk for overwhelming infection and what vaccination should he have received?','["A. Asplenic patients cannot produce T-cell responses to encapsulated bacteria; MMR vaccine is indicated","B. The spleen is required for opsonisation and phagocytosis of encapsulated organisms; pneumococcal vaccine (PCV13 and PPSV23) should have been given","C. Splenectomy eliminates natural killer cells; influenza vaccine is the most important preventive measure","D. Asplenic patients are deficient in IgA; intravenous immunoglobulin is the primary prevention"]','B','The spleen is a critical organ for filtering and phagocytosing encapsulated bacteria (Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae type b, Neisseria meningitidis). Asplenic patients are highly susceptible to overwhelming post-splenectomy infection (OPSI), predominantly from S. pneumoniae. Prevention: (1) pneumococcal vaccination with PCV13 (conjugate) followed by PPSV23 (polysaccharide); (2) Hib vaccine; (3) meningococcal vaccine; (4) lifelong prophylactic penicillin V; (5) patient education. Vaccines should ideally be given ≥2 weeks before elective splenectomy or as soon as possible after emergency splenectomy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Oxford Handbook of Respiratory Medicine 3rd Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Hospital-Acquired & Aspiration Pneumonia','medium','mcq','A 66-year-old woman is admitted to a surgical ward following a laparotomy. On day 3 she develops fever, new oxygen requirement, and a productive cough. CXR shows left lower lobe consolidation. Which of the following is the most important risk factor for developing early-onset HAP in this patient?','["A. Female sex","B. Post-operative state with reduced cough reflex, aspiration risk, and impaired mucociliary clearance","C. Age over 60 years as an independent risk factor","D. Amoxicillin prophylaxis given pre-operatively"]','B','Post-operative patients are at high risk of early-onset HAP due to multiple concurrent factors: impaired cough reflex from analgesia and sedation, supine positioning, aspiration of oropharyngeal secretions, pain limiting deep breathing, and mucociliary clearance impairment from anaesthetic gases and endotracheal intubation. Microaspiration of colonised oropharyngeal secretions is the primary mechanism for HAP. Early-onset HAP (<4 days) is caused by organisms similar to CAP. Pre-operative antibiotic prophylaxis prevents surgical site infection but does not prevent HAP.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','IDSA Clinical Practice Guidelines for HAP and VAP 2016'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Tuberculosis — Diagnosis','medium','mcq','A 52-year-old man presents with chronic back pain and progressive difficulty walking. MRI spine reveals vertebral body destruction of T10–T11 with a paravertebral abscess and cord compression. He has a history of weight loss and night sweats. What is the most likely diagnosis and what is the eponymous name for this spinal condition?','["A. Pyogenic vertebral osteomyelitis (Staphylococcus aureus)","B. Spinal tuberculosis — Pott''s disease","C. Metastatic spinal cord compression from occult malignancy","D. Brucella spondylitis"]','B','Pott''s disease (tuberculous spondylitis) is a form of skeletal extrapulmonary TB affecting the vertebral bodies, most commonly the lower thoracic and upper lumbar spine (T10–L2). Features: vertebral body destruction (anterior more than posterior), disc space narrowing, paravertebral (cold) abscess, and gibbus deformity. Neurological complications from cord compression are a surgical emergency. MRI is the investigation of choice. Treatment: standard 2RHZE/4RH (9–12 months for spinal TB). Surgical decompression may be needed for cord compromise.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Tuberculosis — Treatment','medium','mcq','A 30-year-old man is diagnosed with latent TB infection (LTBI) after a positive IGRA test. He is a close contact of a smear-positive pulmonary TB case. He has no symptoms and his CXR is normal. Active TB has been excluded. Which of the following is a recommended preventive therapy (PT) regimen for LTBI?','["A. 2 months rifampicin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide, and ethambutol (2RHZE)","B. 6 months isoniazid alone (6H) or 3 months rifampicin plus isoniazid (3RH)","C. 12 months isoniazid alone is the only WHO-approved regimen","D. LTBI requires no treatment in contacts under 35 years of age"]','B','WHO-recommended LTBI preventive therapy (PT) regimens include: (1) 6 months isoniazid (6H); (2) 9 months isoniazid (9H); (3) 3 months rifampicin + isoniazid (3RH); (4) 4 months rifampicin alone (4R); or (5) 3 months weekly isoniazid + rifapentine (3HP). The 3RH and 4R regimens have better completion rates and lower hepatotoxicity than 6H. Active TB must always be excluded before starting LTBI treatment, as monotherapy risks selecting drug-resistant strains if active disease is present.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','WHO Consolidated Guidelines on Tuberculosis 2022'),
